=== entry a50d8be3e67e
kind: translation
rules: delegate, team-kit, verify-surface, cursor-only
intent: Translates the trigger list. Every agent is a `delegate_task` child, so the "Task subagent or Project worker" split folds into one spawn path whose `task` opens with the worker.md line; a `poteto-agent` also gets the prompt-file line and this file's path the Subagents section gives. **deslop** is optional with the plugin name dropped, UI and CLI shipping drives the project's verification skill or T3's preview tools and the shell, and the clause warning off Cursor's built-in babysit skill is dropped. Every other trigger stays verbatim.
--- upstream
- First message → restate the user's goal and the smallest change that meets it, then proceed. Open each subagent brief, PR `## Why`, and final report with the goal.
- Starting any agent, whether a Task subagent or a Project worker → the first line of its kickoff is `Read <path>/worker.md before anything else.`, with `<path>` the absolute path of this skill's folder. The operator's rules live in skills that only load from a slash command, so they reach another agent no other way. Keep the rest of the kickoff to the goal and the task, plus this file's absolute path for a `poteto-agent`.
- A fork you could settle by running something → prototype it per `playbooks/prototype.md` instead of asking.
- Code → name the data shape first. Across a function boundary → **architect**. Fan-out → **swarm**. Bakeoff → **arena**. Contested design → **interrogate**.
- Prose, your reply included → **unslop**. Docs, PR descriptions, commit messages → **technical-writing**.
- Before commit → `/deslop` from the `cursor-team-kit` plugin. Before review → **no-comments**.
- Shipping UI / IDE / CLI → `control-ui` or `control-cli` from `cursor-team-kit`.
- A number you measured → **benchmark-checklist**.
- PR status → the **Babysit** playbook, not Cursor's built-in babysit skill. Landing a green stack → the **Shipping** playbook.
- Bot review comments → judge each on its merits.
- Long or unattended work → **show-me-your-work**.
--- patched
- First message → restate the user's goal and the smallest change that meets it, then proceed. Open each subagent brief, PR `## Why`, and final report with the goal.
- Starting any agent with `delegate_task` → the first line of its `task` is `Read <path>/worker.md before anything else.`, with `<path>` the absolute path of this skill's folder. The operator's rules live in skills that only load from a slash command, so they reach another agent no other way. Keep the rest of the kickoff to the goal and the task, plus, for a `poteto-agent`, the prompt-file line and this file's absolute path from the Subagents section.
- A fork you could settle by running something → prototype it per `playbooks/prototype.md` instead of asking.
- Code → name the data shape first. Across a function boundary → **architect**. Fan-out → **swarm**. Bakeoff → **arena**. Contested design → **interrogate**.
- Prose, your reply included → **unslop**. Docs, PR descriptions, commit messages → **technical-writing**.
- Before commit → **deslop**, if it is installed. Before review → **no-comments**.
- Shipping UI / IDE / CLI → the project's own verification skill for that surface, if it has one. Otherwise drive a UI with T3's `preview_*` tools (`preview_snapshot` with `save: true` for a screenshot the user can see), and a CLI or TUI from the shell.
- A number you measured → **benchmark-checklist**.
- PR status → the **Babysit** playbook. Landing a green stack → the **Shipping** playbook.
- Bot review comments → judge each on its merits.
- Long or unattended work → **show-me-your-work**.

=== entry 87699a4e00ff
kind: translation
rules: agent-brief, invoke
intent: Playbook-step subagents are briefed to read the poteto-agent prompt file (resolved to an absolute path) and given this skill's path, right after the worker.md line, since T3 has no agent type field. Preserves that the receiving chat runs the playbook itself and that routed skills keep their own briefs. The chat that runs the playbook is named by the skill the user started, with no slash.
--- upstream
**Use `subagent_type: "poteto-agent"` for any subagent you spawn directly inside a playbook step.** The chat that receives `/poteto-mode` runs the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`interrogate`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type`. Respect it.
--- patched
**Brief any subagent you spawn directly inside a playbook step as a `poteto-agent`.** Resolve `../pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` (relative to this skill's folder) and this file to absolute paths. After the worker.md line, the brief says "Read `<abs>/pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` and act as that agent. The poteto-mode skill is at `<abs>/poteto-mode/SKILL.md`." The chat where the user starts the **poteto-mode** skill runs the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`interrogate`, `swarm`) set their own agent briefs. Respect them.

=== entry 88f38a30b2bb
kind: translation
rules: delegate, background, read-only, capabilities, general-purpose, model-role, invoke
intent: Spawn defaults in T3 terms: async `delegate_task` with the whole brief and its own request ID, no `runtimeMode`, and models from the project models file (written by the setup-pstack skill, named with no slash) or willyskills's role defaults, applied per the model-entry snippet. "Project workers included" is folded in, since every T3 agent is a `delegate_task` child. The `inherit-parent`/`auto` clause about spawning `generalPurpose` because `poteto-agent` pins a model is dropped (the snippet covers `inherit-parent`, and a T3 brief pins no model), and so is the `# defaults: 3` marker check, which detects Cursor-era files holding stale slugs and has no T3 counterpart. Preserves which line each kind of work reads, including trivial mechanical edits on `swarm workers`.
--- upstream
**Defaults for every `Task` call.** `run_in_background: true`, agent mode (readonly strips MCP), file pointers not inlined context, and an explicit model per role, Project workers included. `/setup-pstack` writes `~/.cursor/rules/pstack-models.mdc`, whose per-role lines override the defaults below and the role models in the routed skills (`arena`, `swarm`, `interrogate`). A role with no line keeps its default. A line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs the role on the parent model, so omit `model` and spawn `generalPurpose`, because `poteto-agent` defaults to Opus 5.5. Each code playbook reads its own line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`, and trivial mechanical edits read `swarm workers`. If the rule has no `# defaults: 3` line, an older setup wrote it. Keep honoring its lines, and tell the user once per session to rerun `/setup-pstack`.
--- patched
**Defaults for every `delegate_task` call.** `mode: "async"`, the inherited runtime mode (never set `runtimeMode`, a narrower mode makes the child wait for approvals no one can give), the whole brief in `task` with file pointers not inlined context, its own `clientRequestId` (reused only to retry that spawn), and an explicit model per role. Call `orchestrator_capabilities` once before the first spawn. Keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. The child's completion wakes this thread, so end the turn instead of polling, and use `task_status` only for a result needed mid-turn. The **setup-pstack** skill writes {{snippet:models-file}}, whose per-role lines override the defaults below and the role models in the routed skills (`arena`, `swarm`, `interrogate`). A role with no line keeps its default. {{snippet:model-entry}} Each code playbook reads its own line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`, and trivial mechanical edits read `swarm workers`.

=== entry c1ab5af5119c
kind: translation
rules: model-role
intent: The default list names each role's willyskills default through `{{model:...}}` instead of hand-written slugs. Code lists each code playbook's role, since willyskills's code roles have separate defaults, and the three-model panel becomes one runner per entry of the panel role's line.
--- upstream
- Code, prose, and judgment: `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-fast`.
- Trivial mechanical edits: `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast`.
- Diverse review and design panels: one runner each on `claude-fable-5-2-high`, `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast`, and `gemini-4-neon-xhigh`.
--- patched
- Code: `feature, refactoring` {{model:feature, refactoring}}, `bug-fix` {{model:bug-fix}}, `perf-issue` {{model:perf-issue}}, `hillclimb` {{model:hillclimb}}.
- Prose and judgment: {{model:judgment and prose}}.
- Trivial mechanical edits: {{model:swarm workers}}.
- Diverse review and design panels: one runner per entry of the panel's line, `arena runners` {{model:arena runners}} and `interrogate reviewers` {{model:interrogate reviewers}}.

=== entry ce7bd647a16c
kind: translation
rules: model-role, model-unnamed
intent: Upstream names Fable 5.2 as a premium model kept for panels and for work two top-effort runs of the default code model failed. willyskills has no Fable role, so the strongest model maps to the `hardest tasks` role (rule model-unnamed: strongest judgment model), and the two failed runs are on the task's ordinary role (its playbook's line) at that line's full effort, matching upstream's two runs of the default code model at its top level. No rule covers renaming a named model's escalation gate. The gate itself (user named it, or two failed runs, say which, difficulty alone never qualifies) is kept.
--- upstream
**Fable 5.2 is an exception.** It runs outside a panel only when the user named it for this task or two Opus 5.5 xhigh runs of the same brief failed its check. Say which. Difficulty alone never qualifies.
--- patched
**The `hardest tasks` role is an exception.** A spawn uses its line in {{snippet:models-file}}, else {{model:hardest tasks}}, only when the user named it for this task or two runs of the same brief on the task's own role (its playbook's line), at that line's full effort, failed its check. Say which. Difficulty alone never qualifies.

=== entry 0753d7b8daac
kind: translation
rules: model-effort, model-role, same-model-review, invoke
intent: The role's effort is a ceiling: keep it for the hardest work, lower it for judgment and routine work, never raise it, and never set `fastMode`. Preserves that panel seats keep their listed effort and that only effort changes (the role's model stays). Verification runs on the same model and effort as the worker it checks, unless the user named another (policy same-model-review), instead of upstream's fixed `swarm workers` model. Drops the always-fast rule for one model, the per-model effort cap for Gemini, and the rejected-slug fallback chain. The setup-pstack skill is named with no slash.
--- upstream
**Match the model to the job.** Start from the role's line or default, keep its family, and set the level the job needs. The `/setup-pstack` budget sets each role's starting level. Panel seats keep their listed level. Raise any other role one step, up to xhigh, for the hardest or subtlest work, lower it one step for judgment, and two or more steps for routine work. No role runs above xhigh. Opus 5.5 always runs its `-fast` slug. Verification always runs on the `swarm workers` model, `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast` by default. A Gemini seat runs at `gemini-4-neon-xhigh` unless its line names another level. If the Task tool rejects a slug, never pass it again. Fall back to the role's line or default, then the calling skill's fallback, then the closest valid slug of the same family, then omit `model`. Say so.
--- patched
**Match the model to the job.** Start from the role's line or default, keep its model, and set the effort the job needs. The **setup-pstack** skill's budget sets each role's starting effort, and that effort is a ceiling. Panel seats keep their listed effort. Keep any other role's effort for the hardest or subtlest work, lower it one step for judgment, and two or more steps for routine work. Never raise it, and never set `fastMode`. Verification runs on the same model and effort as the worker it checks, unless the user named another. If a model has no effort option at a level, use its nearest level below. `orchestrator_capabilities` lists each model's options.

=== entry 690c716cedc6
kind: policy
rules: same-model-review
intent: Applies policy same-model-review: a second opinion is a fresh run of the same prompt on the same model and effort, unless the user names another, instead of another vendor's model. It still applies while upstream asks for a second opinion outside the arena and interrogate panels.
--- upstream
You own every subagent's work. Review the diff and write your own summary. A second opinion is the same prompt on another vendor's model.
--- patched
You own every subagent's work. Review the diff and write your own summary. A second opinion is a fresh run of the same prompt on the same model and effort, unless the user names another.

=== entry 1ad3e0bea581
kind: translation
rules: todolist
intent: The visible step list is `update_plan` in Codex and a list kept in the reply in Claude Code. Preserves that the playbook's steps are copied in first and the routing to figure-it-out.
--- upstream
Match the task to a playbook and open its steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case. Copy its steps into the todolist tool first. Large, cross-cutting, or unattended work, or a task no playbook fits, routes to the **figure-it-out** skill.
--- patched
Match the task to a playbook and open its steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case. Copy its steps into a step list first. Codex keeps it with `update_plan`. Claude Code keeps it in the reply and updates it at each phase. Large, cross-cutting, or unattended work, or a task no playbook fits, routes to the **figure-it-out** skill.

